    {
      "dom_id": "question-137761",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14196242/assets/675a66c94bee0deba216bf684aa2b74e6fa048ed62a3944dabe50cdbb88952f3.png)\n\nEach of the six shapes in the model above represents one whole. From left to right, what numbers are missing from the division problem below?\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\,\\,}÷\\frac{1}{3}=\\boxed{\\,\\,}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "In the model, there are $6$ shapes. Each shape consists of $3$ pieces that each represent $\\frac{1}{3}$ of a whole. There are $18$ pieces in total.\n\nThe model tells us that when we divide $6$ wholes into thirds, we will be left with $18$ pieces.\n\nHence, the division problem is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\,6\\,}÷\\frac{1}{3}=\\boxed{\\,18\\,}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing numbers are $6$ and $18.$",
